# Frontend Rules

## Project

The codebase must stay simple, explicit, and maintainable.

## Mandatory Stack

Use only this stack unless explicitly instructed otherwise:

- React
- Vite
- TypeScript
- Effector
- Atomic Router
- Yarn
- Feature-Sliced Design
- React Hook Form
- Zod

## Forbidden Technologies

Do not introduce:

- styled-components
- shadcn/ui
- Redux
- MobX
- Zustand
- Recoil
- React Router
- Next.js
- SCSS architecture
- Tailwind CSS
- npm
- pnpm
- bun

## Styling

- When the project has no established UI library or styling approach, use plain CSS Modules (`*.module.css`) for component styles. Do not add a UI library solely for styling.
- Keep each stylesheet next to its component inside the owning FSD slice and apply classes through `className`.
- Use shared CSS custom properties for design tokens such as colors, spacing, typography, radii, and shadows. Reuse existing tokens before adding new ones.
- Keep global CSS limited to resets, font declarations, design tokens, themes, and base document styles. Do not put component-specific styles there.
- Use CSS classes for static styles and variants. Use inline styles only for values computed at runtime, preferably by passing CSS custom properties.
- Keep selectors local and shallow; avoid IDs, `!important`, and selectors that depend on another component's internal markup.
- Include responsive layouts, visible keyboard focus, and appropriate hover, disabled, and error states where applicable.
- Reuse existing UI components before creating new ones. CSS Modules provide styling, not accessible control behavior; preserve semantic HTML and keyboard interaction.
- In an existing project, follow its established styling approach. Do not introduce a second styling system or migrate existing components unless explicitly requested.

## General Principles

- Prefer readable code over clever code.
- Prefer explicit implementation over hidden magic.
- Do not create abstractions before they are needed.
- Do not generate future-proof infrastructure without a real use case.
- Do not duplicate existing logic, entities, features, or components.
- Do not make architectural decisions without explicit instruction.
- Generate only what was requested.

## TypeScript

- Use TypeScript.
- Avoid `any`.
- If `any` is temporarily unavoidable, add a TODO comment explaining why.
- Prefer explicit domain types.
- Prefer `interface` for domain models.
- Prefer `type` for utility types, unions, and mapped types.
- When a Zod schema defines the data shape, infer the type from the schema instead of creating a duplicate interface or type.

## Data Access

- UI components must not import JSON mock files directly.
- Access API data through `shared/api` services.

## Code Quality

- One file should have one main responsibility.
- Keep files small and focused.
- Avoid files larger than 300 lines.
- Avoid deeply nested JSX.
- Keep business logic out of React components.
- Keep UI components presentation-focused.
- Reuse existing code before creating new code.
